2.3.1 Recruitment
To implement guided self-help interventions using the support model described for SbS and DWM, you will need to recruit helpers, supervisors and trainers. Because the helper's role is to support people through the intervention, not to provide specialized mental health support, they do not need to be highly educated or experienced mental health workers. They could for example be nonspecialist community workers, educators, students or graduates. These helpers will need training in the guided self-help intervention they are supporting.
When recruiting non-specialist helpers, you will want to engage people who:
- have a genuine motivation to help others;
- can work or have previously worked with people who may be in distress;
- have an adequate educational background to manage programme materials (for example, have completed at least high school);
- speak the same language and ideally have a similar cultural background to service users;
- are interested in learning how to provide support as a helper;
- can access ongoing support and supervision from a trained supervisor; and
- have enough time available to deliver the self-help.
All these characteristics support non-specialist helpers to excel at their role. For information on other attitudes, skills and experiences to look for when recruiting helpers, see section 4.1 of WHO's Psychological interventions implementation manual (29).
Health professionals such as doctors and nurses in primary health care can also provide guided self-help, although the support model they follow may need adjusting, for example by integrating support into routine follow-up appointments.
Helpers experienced in providing psychological interventions (for example, mental health professionals or people who have delivered Problem Management Plus) should still complete all training activities. However, they can probably complete the training more quickly than inexperienced helpers, as they already understand basic helping skills and know how to identify and respond to imminent risk of suicide and other safety or protection issues. Ongoing supervision is still required.

Step 2. Further assessment
Individuals who are suitable for the intervention and want to take part in it can be asked further assessment questions, for example to identify:
- level of well-being or functioning: brief weekly measures to assess symptoms can also be used by helpers to assess ongoing risk, help tailor support and determine if the intervention is helping;
- day and time preferences for support contact (if offered guided self-help);
- brief weekly measures to assess symptoms can also be integrated into the self-help programme to guide helpers in tailoring support (if offered guided self-help).
Make sure that any further assessment is not too long, so that people remain engaged in the intervention.

3.1.1 Roles and responsibilities
As a helper you will:
- welcome each service user to the intervention;
- guide people through the intervention, helping them to engage, learn and use the self-help skills and exercises in their everyday life (including by answering questions or clarifying techniques and listening to other problems they are experiencing);
- make regular contact with all service users (typically 15 minutes each week for DWM and SbS) by phone, video or message, as determined by your organization;
- follow the intervention;
- maintain service users' privacy and safely keep case notes and contact records, as requested by your organization (see Annex 5. Case note examples for example case note templates);
- listen to service users and manage safety concerns according to established protocols (with supervision and support) (Box 3.1);
- attend and actively participate in regular supervision;
- adhere to the principles and processes outlined in this manual;
- manage your time and schedule for contacting service users; and
- maintain your own well-being, by accepting support from your supervisor and peers and by prioritizing self-care activities.
If you are helping with a digital intervention, as well as the list above, you may also be expected to give service users technical support on, for example, obtaining a user name, resetting a password and navigating the intervention (for example, how to access exercises or move on to the next module). Your organization will tell you the process for this.
You do not need to be an expert in the theory behind guided self-help and should not get into long discussions about the therapeutic techniques and activities, since this is provided by the intervention itself.

Don't …
- be honest and trustworthy;
- be sensitive and empathetic;
- respect people's rights to make their own decisions;
- be aware of and set aside personal biases, judgements and prejudices;
- make it clear to people that even if they refuse help now, they can still access help later;
- respect privacy and keep people's stories confidential;
- know protocols and referral pathways for people who need more or other support;
- behave appropriately regardless of a person's culture, religion, age or gender.
- exploit your relationship as a helper;
- ask people for money or favours;
- make false promises or give false information;
- give the impression you are a specialized therapist or providing therapy;
- provide advice beyond your role;
- force help on people, or be intrusive or pushy;
- pressure people to tell their story;
- judge a person's actions or feelings.

Stage II: Classroom training
During classroom training, through teaching and role play you will:
- learn basic helping skills (see section 3.2 What helping skills do I need to be a helper?); and
- learn about mental health and presentations of distress, and how they affect people's wellbeing and functioning (the type and extent of this learning will be decided by your organization).
You will also learn how to:
- support people through the intervention;
- identify and manage safety concerns;
- build up trust and confidence within a short time;
- manage the limitations of your role;
- use the digital platform (if relevant); and
- keep a record of contacts with users.
If you are providing support remotely, you should receive extra training in how to do this. During training, you will develop the competencies you need through role play and other learning activities.

Don't …
- respect the person's privacy and confidentiality;
- let the person know you are listening (if using a telephone say "hmm …" or "I see";
- if using messaging, acknowledge what the person said in their last message and always personalize replies);
- be patient and calm;
- give accurate information;
- be honest about what you know and don't know (for example, "I don't know, but I will try to find out about that for you");
- give information in a way the person can understand - keep it simple;
- acknowledge how the person is feeling and any losses or important events they tell you about;
- acknowledge the person's strengths;
- respect the person's beliefs or spirituality;
- politely steer the conversation away if the person asks you personal questions;
- if using a telephone, call from a quiet place to talk and minimize outside distractions, and allow for some silence in the conversation.
- pressure the person into telling you anything;
- interrupt or rush the person when they are speaking;
- judge what the person has or hasn't done or how they feel;
- make up things you don't know;
- use terms that are too technical or stigmatizing;
- tell the person someone else's story;
- talk about your own troubles;
- give false promises;
- try to solve the person's problems for them;
- if using written messages, don't use emojis or abbreviations (for example, write "you" instead of "u").

4.1 Welcome contact
Your first contact with a service user is different to the others. It is a welcome call or message and happens after someone has signed up to the intervention. This contact is an opportunity to provide more information about the intervention, to introduce yourself and to establish how you will work together. This may help reduce drop-out later on. See Annex 1A. Summary of tasks for welcome contact for a summary of tasks for the welcome contact and Annex 1B. Example script and text for welcome contact for example scripts and text.
During the welcome contact, you will:
- welcome the person to the intervention and briefly introduce yourself;
- tell them about the intervention (including that it requires practice between modules);
- (for a guided intervention) explain what they can expect from you as a helper - tell them you are not a mental health specialist and explain what they can expect of you as their helper;
- (if there is an option of guidance, but they have not chosen it) explain what the guidance is like and that they can always switch to the guidance option;
- explain confidentiality and its limitations;
- give them the opportunity to ask any questions;
- (for a guided intervention) remind them of the day and time of your next contact; and
- say goodbye.
The aim of the welcome contact is to introduce the person to the intervention and yourself, to manage expectations and to help them feel more at ease. Remember to read through any information given to you (such as referral information) before you make the welcome contact.

4.9 How do I deal with other challenges?
There are many reasons why a support contact may become challenging. For example, the service user may:
- experience distress during a contact;
- talk about an overwhelming number of other problems in their life;
- need extensive support coping with external problems;
- continue talking after 15 minutes or send very long messages;
- report problems with completing the intervention's exercises or home practice;
- lack motivation;
- not respect boundaries;
- have a friend or family member who calls to ask about the intervention; or
- wishes to stop doing the intervention.
See Annex 2. Troubleshooting templates for a short troubleshooting guide to each of these challenges. This guide is not an exhaustive list but you can always consult your supervisor when you are experiencing challenges.

5.1 Overview
SbS is a brief online self-help intervention for adults with depression (49). It was developed by WHO in collaboration with other organizations. 7 Comprising WHO model, it has gone through extensive peer review, was tested in two RCTs in Lebanon and has been running as a national service in Lebanon since 2022 (see Box 1.1 Step-by-Step in Lebanon).
SbS:
- is proven to improve symptoms of depression, anxiety, functioning, post-traumatic stress, subjective well-being and self-identified personal problems;
- can be offered alongside other mental health interventions, or community programming;
- can be delivered remotely and at scale (to many people at the same time);
- provides psychoeducation and training in behavioural activation alongside other therapeutic techniques such as slow breathing, identifying strengths, positive self-talk, increasing social support and relapse prevention;
- is delivered using a fictional story narrated by people seeking help for depression, with a trusted character (such as a health worker) giving instructions on therapeutic techniques; and
- encourages people to practise techniques on their own, in between modules.
7 WHO collaborators were the National Mental Health Programme at the Ministry of Public Health in Lebanon, Freie Universität Berlin and University of Zurich.
SbS content can be added to any online platform (for example, an e-learning platform) and can be adapted for use in other media formats such as books or videos. SbS can also be delivered as an unguided intervention; this might make the intervention less effective, but it will be able to reach more people.
If delivered through a digital platform, people receiving SbS will need a compatible device (smartphone, tablet or computer) and a reliable internet connection to complete it. They may also need headphones to listen to the intervention's audio exercises. Using SbS on a platform that can be used offline (such as an app) is probably preferable in some settings. In all cases, implementation requires sufficient IT knowledge and resources to host and maintain the platform and ensure good security.
When offered with guidance, telephone, internet-based calls, video support or instant messaging will be required.

6.1 Overview
DWM is a brief self-help intervention that is one part of WHO's group stress management intervention, Self-Help Plus (9, 31). DWM can also be delivered as an individual self-help intervention. It has been used in research trials as a web application (50) and found to be effective using five support contacts. In addition, the book version of DWM has been found to be effective in a pilot RCT (16) using three phone support contacts, and in a study in Iraq using three face-to-face support contacts (24). Results from unpublished field tests with over 500 people in Syrian Arab Republic and Türkiye showed significant reductions in distress for people using DWM.
DWM is:
- based on acceptance and commitment therapy and adapted for many settings;
- intended as an intervention for managing stress and coping with adversity;
- evaluated for use with people aged over 18 years;
- designed to be completed over a short period of time;
- practical, so that people can practise skills regularly on their own; and
- illustrated, with accompanying audio recordings to support regular practice.
DWM content is publicly available as an illustrated book in a number of languages (9), and in a more limited number of languages as a web app (https://dwmatters.eu). WHO has a translation and adaptation package available on request that contains all DWM content (text and illustrations) and supports new translations or implementations. 8
DWM can be provided in different ways, including by lay providers with weekly support contacts and by primary health care providers with follow-up as part of routine appointments. It can also be provided as unguided self-help, by making the book or web app accessible to people.
In the book version of DWM, each chapter introduces a skill, summarizes key tools and activities, and provides guidance and audio exercises to help people practise them.

Annex 2C. Service user needs support coping
If external problems are preventing someone from progressing with the intervention, you can support their coping strategies. In this situation, you can spend a few minutes helping the person to identify their own coping strategies and support, following the four steps below.
When discussing external problems remember to:
- ask open-ended questions;
- not rush into providing suggestions too quickly;
- give the person time to talk and think for themselves.

Annex 2I. Service user (or a family member) wants to stop the intervention
People can be unhappy about or stop engaging with the intervention for many reasons, for example:
- they are not sure why they are completing the intervention;
- they do not understand the ideas presented;
- they do not like the intervention or are bored by the content; or
- they are experiencing other problems in their lives.
